/*!
    \internal
    \page qml-name-resolution.html
    \title Resolving Unqualified Names in QML

    This page specifies how the QML engine and the QML compiler resolve unqualified names.

    \section1 Terminology

    \list
    \li The \e location is the place in a document where the name to be resolved occurs.
    \li An \e {unqualified name} is an identifier that is not the member of an object expression,
        that is, not preceded by a \c{.}, and that no JavaScript declaration resolves. The
        declarations are local variables, function parameters, and function names. They resolve
        names lexically, before any of the rules below. In
        \c{function f() { let Infinity = 42; return Infinity }}, \c Infinity is not an
        unqualified name, and the function returns 42. In \c{property int i: width}, \c width is
        an unqualified name.
    \li An \e id is the name given to an object with an \c id property, such as \c root in
        \c{id: root}. The engine stores the ids of a component as context properties of the
        component's \c QQmlContext.
    \li The \e {immediate scope object} is the object whose binding, signal handler, or function
        contains the location. In the example below, it is the \c Rectangle for the binding on
        \c color and the \c Text for the binding on \c text.
    \li The \e {immediate context} is the \c QQmlContext of the component that contains the
        location. Its \e {context object} is typically the root object of that component.
        Often, the scope object and the context object are the same.
    \li The \e {lexically enclosing contexts} are the contexts of the components in whose
        definitions the location is nested, from the inside out. In the example below, the
        delegate of \c Repeater is a component defined inside \c Outer.qml. A location inside the
        delegate has the delegate context as immediate context. The \c Outer.qml context is
        lexically enclosing. A context in which \c Outer or the delegate is merely instantiated
        is not.
    \endlist

    \qml
    // Outer.qml
    Item {                              // context object of the Outer.qml context
        id: outerRoot
        Repeater {
            delegate: Item {            // context object of the delegate context
                id: delegateRoot
                Rectangle {
                    id: inner
                    color: delegateRoot.color   // scope object: the Rectangle
                    Text { text: outerRoot.objectName }  // scope object: the Text
                }
            }
        }
    }
    \endqml

    "Lookup" has a specific meaning in the engine, which is not invoked by these general rules.

    \section1 Name Resolution Rules

    The rules below are checked in order. A rule applies if the place it describes has a member of
    the given name, no matter whether that member is a property, method, signal, id, type, import
    namespace, or imported script. The first rule that applies decides. We don't need to know the
    kind of a name beforehand. In particular, a method found in an earlier place shadows a property
    of the same name in a later place, and the other way around.

    \list 1
    \li \target rule-keywords Keywords: The translation functions and the constants \c undefined, \c
        Infinity, and \c NaN override everything else, in translation bindings as well as in calls
        at run time.
    \li \target rule-imports Imports: Types, import namespaces, and imported scripts of the document
        containing the location. Upper-case type names are always found. Lower-case type names
        (value types) are only found with \c{ValueTypeBehavior: Addressable}. The precedence between
        different kinds of imports (explicit module, directory, or script; scoped or unscoped;
        implicit; qmldir) and the impact of their ordering in a document shall be specified
        separately.
    \li \target rule-immediate-ids Ids of the immediate context. Only a QQmlExpression evaluated in
        a context created by the user, for example for a script string, can find context properties
        other than ids here.
    \li \target rule-bound-ids With \c{ComponentBehavior: Bound}: Ids of the lexically enclosing
        contexts, from the inside out. The chain ends at the root object of the document. Inline
        components are exempt from \c{ComponentBehavior: Bound} because they can be instantiated
        anywhere, for example as \c Doc.Inner in a different document. Therefore, the root object of
        an inline component ends the chain, too. The context an inline component is instantiated in
        is never lexically enclosing, even if it belongs to the same document. All of these contexts
        are internal and can therefore not contain custom context properties. \c{ComponentBehavior:
        Bound} causes Qt's own types not to insert any further contexts between a component and its
        creation context.
    \li \target rule-scope-object The immediate scope object.
    \li \target rule-context-object The context object of the immediate context.
    \li \target rule-bound-context-objects With \c{ComponentBehavior: Bound}: The context objects of
        the lexically enclosing contexts, in the same order as in \l {rule-bound-ids}{rule 4}.
    \li \target rule-global-object The remaining members of the JavaScript global object, see \l
        {rule-keywords}{rule 1} for the others.
    \li \target rule-remaining-contexts The remaining contexts, outside the ones covered in \l
        {rule-immediate-ids}{rule 3}, \l {rule-bound-ids}{4}, \l {rule-context-object}{6}, and \l
        {rule-bound-context-objects}{7}. For each of those, the context properties are searched
        first, then the context object.
    \endlist

    All of this holds no matter if the code is compiled to C++ or interpreted, and no matter if
    the \c{Lookups} are populated already.

    \section1 Disappearing Scope and Context Objects

    If a scope object or a context object disappears at run time, its type is still retained and
    we can still determine whether it would have resolved the name in question. If so, a
    ReferenceError is thrown. The properties of the object's context, typically ids, stay in
    place. An id whose object is gone is null. A scope object can disappear while code using it as
    scope is still alive, for example a function stored in a \c var property after the object it
    is defined in has been destroyed.

    \section1 Internal, Frozen, and User-Created Contexts

    The contexts created by the QML engine for the components it instantiates are internal. They
    cannot gain or lose properties and their context objects cannot be replaced.

    Qt's own types that insert contexts between an unbound component and its creation context
    (Loader, the delegate models, ListView's section delegates, PageIndicator's delegates, and some
    dialog implementations) shall freeze those contexts after filling them, using a new public
    method on QQmlContext. A frozen context behaves like an internal one.

    User code can still insert contexts that are not frozen between an unbound component and its
    creation context, using a QQmlContext and QQmlComponent::create() or similar. Such contexts are
    only searched in \l {rule-remaining-contexts}{rule 9}, and replacements of their context objects
    can be of a different type. We cannot see such contexts ahead of time. Therefore, qmlcachegen
    compiles code as if they were not present.

    \section1 Dynamic Metaobjects

    A scope object or context object with a dynamic metaobject can gain and lose names at any
    time. The only public way to create such an object is QQmlPropertyMap. Qt's own model items
    also have dynamic metaobjects.

    \section1 Caching and Compiling Ahead of Time

    Based on the rules above, the following guidelines apply to caching and compiling ahead of
    time:

    \list
    \li Keywords, types, and the global object cannot change. They can be compiled ahead of time
        and cached in lookups. Ids, properties, and methods declared in QML cannot be named like
        members of the global object.
    \li The ids of a document are fixed. A context with ids is internal and cannot gain or lose
        properties. Therefore, the ids, including the enclosing ids of a bound component, can be
        compiled ahead of time and cached.
    \li The types of the scope object and the context object are known when compiling ahead of
        time. Only script strings and PropertyChanges can run the same code with a different scope
        object or context object, and those are not compiled to C++. On the lookup side, deep
        lookups into the context hierarchy are only cached as
        \c ContextGetterInParentContextHierarchy anyway and those are repeated each time.
        Therefore, checking the top context or scope object for changes is enough. If we detect an
        object with a dynamic metaobject or a non-internal immediate context, we can't cache
        anything beyond those since they might gain or lose properties at any time, invalidating
        previous results.
    \li The lexically enclosing contexts of a bound component are internal, and their context
        objects are known when compiling ahead of time. In principle we could cache and compile
        them. Where necessary this can be added. The compiler has to check them before resolving a
        name to the global object, though.
    \li Only QQmlExpression, and therefore script strings, can run in a non-internal immediate
        context (that is, a user-created context).
    \li Anything found on or beyond an object with a dynamic metaobject cannot be compiled ahead
        of time. The compiler only knows its static properties. It can recognize QQmlPropertyMap.
    \li The remaining contexts of \l {rule-remaining-contexts}{rule 9} come after the global object.
        Frozen and internal ones could be cached, but their context objects may have dynamic
        metaobjects, for example the model items of a delegate model. Anything beyond a context that
        is neither frozen nor internal cannot be cached.
    \endlist
*/
